#Highlight

Colouring notlob source for the screen. There is no hand-written
highlighter here: the source is parsed by notlob's own grammar, and the
colours follow the parse tree. What the viewer sees coloured as a claim
is exactly what notlob will run as a claim -- the picture cannot drift
from the language.

The parser keeps every token's position in the source. A highlight is a
list of spans, each one line, a column range, and a kind.

    @dataclass(frozen=True)
    class Span:
        line: int
        start: int
        end: int
        kind: str

##Classification

Most tokens know their kind from their type alone: a module heading is
a title, a cross-reference is a ref. Indented lines are the exception --
the same INDENTED_LINE terminal is code in a code block, a claim body
under a sigil or in the tests section, and a declaration in the
references section -- so for those the enclosing grammar rule decides.

    TOKEN_KINDS = {
        "MOD_HEAD": "title",
        "SUBHEAD": "subheading",
        "LINE_START_TEXT": "prose",
        "PROSE_TEXT": "prose",
        "REF": "ref",
        "BULLET": "bullet",
        "SIGIL": "sigil",
        "TEST_SIGIL": "sigil",
    }

    INDENTED_KINDS = {
        "code_block": "code",
        "claim": "claim",
        "named_test": "claim",
        "test_group": "claim",
        "test_item": "claim",
    }

    def classify(rule: str, token_type: str) -> str:
        if token_type == "INDENTED_LINE":
            return INDENTED_KINDS.get(rule, "structure")
        return TOKEN_KINDS.get(token_type, "structure")

##Spans

Walking the tree pairs each token with the rule that contains it. The
line-oriented terminals consume their own trailing newline; spans stop
short of it, and a blank line contributes no span at all.

    def _terminated(source: str) -> str:
        return source if source.endswith("\n") else source + "\n"

    def _tokens(tree: Tree):
        for child in tree.children:
            if isinstance(child, Tree):
                yield from _tokens(child)
            else:
                yield str(tree.data), child

    def spans(source: str) -> list:
        source = _terminated(source)
        line_starts = [0] + [i + 1 for i, ch in enumerate(source) if ch == "\n"]
        result = []
        for rule, token in _tokens(parse(source)):
            text = source[token.start_pos:token.end_pos].rstrip("\n")
            if not text:
                continue
            line = token.line - 1
            start = token.start_pos - line_starts[line]
            result.append(Span(line, start, start + len(text), classify(rule, token.type)))
        return sorted(result, key=lambda s: (s.line, s.start))

~example
    spans("#Demo\n\nSee #Other here.\n") == [
        Span(0, 0, 5, "title"),
        Span(2, 0, 4, "prose"),
        Span(2, 4, 10, "ref"),
        Span(2, 10, 16, "prose"),
    ]

A highlight is faithful when the spans tile the source: on every line
they are in order, never overlap, leave no gap, and end exactly where
the line ends. Nothing is dropped and nothing is coloured twice.

    def tiles(source: str, found: list) -> bool:
        lines = source.split("\n")
        for index, line in enumerate(lines):
            cursor = 0
            for span in (s for s in found if s.line == index):
                if span.start != cursor:
                    return False
                cursor = span.end
            if cursor != len(line):
                return False
        return True

##Syntax

Inside code, the colours of the language itself: keywords, literals,
comments. Which language is not guessed from file names -- a notlob
project declares it in its binding, so that declaration is read with the
same parser.

    def binding_language(root: Path) -> str:
        tree = parse(_terminated((root / "binding.lob").read_text(encoding="utf-8")))
        for token in tree.scan_values(lambda v: isinstance(v, Token)):
            if token.type == "LANGUAGE_DECL":
                return str(token).split()[1]
        return "text"

~example
    binding_language(Path(".")) == "python"

Syntax is found by lexing whole blocks, never single lines: a string or
comment can run across lines, and only the block knows where it began.
The blocks are the code and claim rules of the parse tree.

    def code_blocks(source: str) -> list:
        blocks = []
        for node in parse(_terminated(source)).iter_subtrees_topdown():
            if str(node.data) in INDENTED_KINDS:
                lines = [c.line - 1 for c in node.children
                         if isinstance(c, Token) and c.type == "INDENTED_LINE"]
                if lines:
                    blocks.append(lines)
        return blocks

Token types from the lexer fold into the few kinds the palette has.

    def syntax_kind(token_type) -> str:
        if token_type in SyntaxToken.Comment:
            return "comment"
        if token_type in SyntaxToken.Keyword:
            return "keyword"
        if token_type in SyntaxToken.Literal:
            return "literal"
        if token_type in SyntaxToken.Operator or token_type in SyntaxToken.Punctuation:
            return "punct"
        return "code"

The lexer reports each token with its offset into the block's text, so
tokens map back to line spans exactly, splitting any that cross a line.

    def syntax_spans(texts: list, line_numbers: list, language: str) -> list:
        text = "\n".join(texts)
        starts = [0]
        for line in texts:
            starts.append(starts[-1] + len(line) + 1)
        lexer = get_lexer_by_name(language, stripnl=False, ensurenl=False)
        result = []
        for offset, token_type, value in lexer.get_tokens_unprocessed(text):
            end = offset + len(value)
            for k, line in enumerate(texts):
                start = max(offset, starts[k])
                stop = min(end, starts[k] + len(line))
                if start < stop:
                    result.append(Span(line_numbers[k], start - starts[k], stop - starts[k],
                                       syntax_kind(token_type)))
        return result

~example
    [s.kind for s in syntax_spans(["return 1"], [0], "python")] == ["keyword", "code", "literal"]

The full highlight keeps notlob's spans for everything outside code and
replaces the flat code and claim spans with the syntax beneath them.

    def highlight(source: str, language: str) -> list:
        lines = _terminated(source).split("\n")
        result = [s for s in spans(source) if s.kind not in ("code", "claim")]
        for block in code_blocks(source):
            result += syntax_spans([lines[i] for i in block], block, language)
        return sorted(result, key=lambda s: (s.line, s.start))

Syntax colouring leaves a claim line looking like any other code, so
the claim lines are recorded separately, to be marked in the margin.

    def claim_lines(source: str) -> set:
        return {s.line for s in spans(source) if s.kind == "claim"}

Everything either layer can say must have a style.

~example
    set(TOKEN_KINDS.values()) | set(INDENTED_KINDS.values()) | {"structure"} <= set(TOKEN_STYLES)
    {syntax_kind(t) for t in (SyntaxToken.Comment, SyntaxToken.Keyword, SyntaxToken.Text)} <= set(TOKEN_STYLES)

Documents are generated from the constructs that matter on screen --
prose, prose with a cross-reference, subheadings, code, claims -- and
every one of them must tile, both as notlob sees it and with syntax.
In this project's harness the first draw can stall for a second or so,
which Hypothesis reports as a slow strategy. The stall is not the
machine waking from sleep: it happens on a machine that has been awake
throughout. Its likely cause is the heavy imports around the property.
Only that timing check is relaxed; every generated document is still
checked.

~property spans-tile-source
    @settings(suppress_health_check=[HealthCheck.too_slow])
    @given(blocks=st.lists(st.one_of(
        st.lists(st.text("abcdefgh", min_size=1, max_size=8), min_size=1, max_size=5).map(" ".join),
        st.text("abcdefgh", min_size=1, max_size=8).map(lambda w: f"see #Petri Net {w}"),
        st.text("abcdefgh", min_size=1, max_size=8).map(lambda w: "##" + w.capitalize()),
        st.tuples(st.text("abc_", min_size=1, max_size=8), st.integers(0, 9999))
          .map(lambda p: f"    {p[0]} = {p[1]}"),
        st.tuples(st.text("abc", min_size=1, max_size=8), st.integers(0, 9999))
          .map(lambda p: f"~example\n    {p[0]} == {p[1]}"),
    ), max_size=8))
    def _(blocks):
        source = "#Generated\n\n" + "\n\n".join(blocks) + "\n"
        assert tiles(source, spans(source))
        assert tiles(source, highlight(source, "python"))

##Typesetting

A listing on screen is always an excerpt, typeset one line at a time.
Three details of manim's text handling shape this. MarkupText would be
the obvious tool, but it hard-codes a Pango wrap width, folding long
lines; plain Text takes per-range colour and font by slice and never
wraps. Word spacing degrades when text is typeset small, proportional
prose most of all, so lines are typeset large and the listing scaled
down afterwards. And a long, multi-line Text mixing many fonts does not
reliably come back with one glyph per character, while single lines
always do.

    TYPESET_SIZE = 72

    def typeset_line(text: str, ranges: list):
        return Text(text, font=CODE_FONT, font_size=TYPESET_SIZE, disable_ligatures=True,
                    t2c={f"[{a}:{b}]": TOKEN_STYLES[kind][0] for a, b, kind in ranges},
                    t2f={f"[{a}:{b}]": TOKEN_STYLES[kind][1] for a, b, kind in ranges})

Lines sit on one grid, prose and code alike. Three measures of the code
font fix it: the advance of one character, which places indentation --
Text trims leading spaces from its bounds, so indent is restored by
column -- the height of a capital, and the pitch from line to line.

    def metrics() -> tuple:
        one = Text("x", font=CODE_FONT, font_size=TYPESET_SIZE)
        eleven = Text("x         x", font=CODE_FONT, font_size=TYPESET_SIZE)
        cap = Text("H", font=CODE_FONT, font_size=TYPESET_SIZE).height
        return (eleven.width - one.width) / 10, cap, cap * 1.9

Each line is set on its baseline, found from its first character that
sits on the baseline without descending. Finding that glyph means
knowing which glyph a character became. Text keeps one glyph per
visible character, in order -- with ligatures off, as they are here --
but where a colour range begins or ends on whitespace it also leaves
empty, sizeless glyphs for the spaces. Only glyphs with extent count,
both for mapping characters and for measuring the line. A line with no
baseline character, a lone closing brace say, is centred on the capital
height instead.

    def visible_glyphs(line) -> list:
        return [g for g in line.submobjects if g.width > 1e-9 or g.height > 1e-9]

    BASELINE_CHARS = frozenset("abcdefhiklmnorstuvwxzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPRSTUVWXYZ0123456789")

    def glyph_indices(text: str) -> dict:
        indices = {}
        for position, ch in enumerate(text):
            if not ch.isspace():
                indices[position] = len(indices)
        return indices

~example
    glyph_indices("a b\nc") == {0: 0, 2: 1, 4: 2}

Placing a line restores its indent by column, then lifts it so its
baseline lands on its row of the grid.

    def place_line(line, text: str, row: int, advance: float, cap: float, pitch: float):
        glyphs = visible_glyphs(line)
        indent = len(text) - len(text.lstrip(" "))
        line.shift(RIGHT * (indent * advance - min(g.get_left()[0] for g in glyphs)))
        baseline = -row * pitch
        anchor = next((p for p, ch in enumerate(text) if ch in BASELINE_CHARS), None)
        if anchor is None or len(glyphs) != len(glyph_indices(text)):
            middle = (min(g.get_bottom()[1] for g in glyphs) + max(g.get_top()[1] for g in glyphs)) / 2
            line.shift(UP * (baseline + cap / 2 - middle))
        else:
            line.shift(UP * (baseline - glyphs[glyph_indices(text)[anchor]].get_bottom()[1]))
        return line

##Claim Bars

Claim lines that follow one another share one bar.

    def runs(lines: list) -> list:
        result = []
        for line in sorted(lines):
            if result and result[-1][1] == line - 1:
                result[-1] = (result[-1][0], line)
            else:
                result.append((line, line))
        return result

~example
    runs([8, 1, 2, 3, 7]) == [(1, 3), (7, 8)]

A bar runs from above the capitals of its first row to below the
descenders of its last, in the margin left of column zero.

    def claim_bar(top: int, bottom: int, advance: float, cap: float, pitch: float):
        x = -CLAIM_BAR_GAP * advance
        return Line([x, -top * pitch + cap * 1.15, 0], [x, -bottom * pitch - cap * 0.35, 0],
                    color=CLAIM, stroke_width=CLAIM_BAR_WIDTH)

##Listings

A listing puts it together: the highlight of the whole source, the
chosen lines typeset and placed on the grid, and bars beside the
claims. Scenes that mark particular lines need the grid as well as the
picture, so typesetting hands back the listing before it is fitted,
its lines by source line number, and the grid's measures.

    def typeset_listing(path: Path, first: int, last: int) -> tuple:
        source = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        found = highlight(source, binding_language(find_project_root(path)))
        lines = source.split("\n")
        grid = metrics()
        rows = {}
        for row, number in enumerate(range(first, last)):
            text = lines[number]
            if text.strip():
                ranges = [(s.start, s.end, s.kind) for s in found if s.line == number]
                rows[number + 1] = place_line(typeset_line(text, ranges), text, row, *grid)
        claims = [n - first for n in claim_lines(source) if first <= n < last]
        bars = [claim_bar(top, bottom, *grid) for top, bottom in runs(claims)]
        return VGroup(*rows.values(), *bars), rows, grid

The plain listing is just that, shrunk to fit the frame.

    def listing(path: Path, first: int, last: int):
        return fit_to_frame(typeset_listing(path, first, last)[0])

---

#Tests

##real sources tile

Every module in the reference projects, and every module of this
project, is highlighted without loss -- by notlob alone, and with the
syntax of its project's declared language.

    all(tiles(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), spans(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))) for p in CORPUS)
    all(tiles(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), highlight(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), binding_language(find_project_root(p)))) for p in CORPUS)

##the star module's cross-reference is found
    Span(4, 5, 15, "ref") in spans(PETRI_MARKING.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

##the star module is TypeScript
    binding_language(find_project_root(PETRI_MARKING)) == "typescript"

#Appendix Corpus

Real notlob sources to check the highlighter against: pn-chomper, the
examples bundled with notlob, and this project's own modules.

    PETRI_MARKING = Path("../ref-projects/pn-chomper/petri/marking.lob")

    CORPUS = sorted(
        list(Path("../ref-projects/pn-chomper").glob("**/*.lob"))
        + list(Path("../ref-projects/notlob/examples").glob("**/*.lob"))
        + list(Path(".").glob("**/*.lob"))
    )

#References
    #Style
    from dataclasses import dataclass
    from pathlib import Path

    from lark import Token, Tree
    from manim import RIGHT, UP, Line, Text, VGroup
    from notlob.parser import parse
    from notlob.project import find_project_root
    from pygments.lexers import get_lexer_by_name
    from pygments.token import Token as SyntaxToken
